WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.160 align:start position:0%
 
White House Budget Director Mick  Mulvaney joins us from Washington good  morning director good morning and thanks  for having me let me ask you when all is  said and done will the Republican health  care plan cover more Americans or less  Americans than Obamacare we really think  it will cover more keep in mind it's  easy to claim success under Obamacare  when you had a government mandate  because you could sort of say well  everybody's covered but is that really  the question the question is not if you  get sick you don't really care if you  have insurance you care whether or not  you can get health care and that's what  we think Obamacare came up so short it  was created a system where it was  affordable to buy insurance but not  affordable to actually go to the doctor  and we think the system that the house  introduced last night that we worked  with very closely with the house and  support actually provides more  affordable health care for people so  when they do get sick they can afford to  go to the doctor I know that you were  hoping to do and across-the-board tax  credit for everyone but the new plan  phases the credits out for individuals  making 75 thousand dollars a year or  families making a hundred and fifty  thousand dollars a year why the change  we just had input from a bunch of  different folks and some people were  uncomfortable as we started the  negotiations with allowing folks who are  making more than that to sort of get  this tax credit what was it designed to  do by the way it's it's only designed to  cover people or to assist people who  don't get coverage at work director one  of the key changes here is that you're  eliminating the government's mandate  that you must have health insurance and  replacing it with a penalty for a lapse  encouraged and again coverage and  increase in premiums how exactly is that  going to work it's called continuous  coverage and it's sort of how we've  proposed to deal with this concept  called adverse selection one of the  problems with Obamacare now is that you  can wait to buy insurance until after  you get sick what we try and do is give  people an incentive to go off and say  look if you're really worried about that  go up and sign up now and make sure that  you can prove that you had continuous  coverage and then if you do get sick you  can't lose your coverage but if you if  you have a penalty kicking in if your  insurance lapse won't that ultimately  discourage people from getting back into  the insurance market no I think the  exact opposite it would encourage people  especially young healthy people one of  the groups  Obamacare was never able to get into the  system it's one of the reason that  Obamacare was such a dismal economic  failure what can you tell us director  about the cost of this plan because  right now the Congressional Budget  Office hasn't reviewed it right we don't  expect the Congressional Budget Office  to give us any numbers for the next  couple of days but at the office of  management budget a couple other  agencies including HHS we've run our own  numbers and were absolutely convinced  because of the efficiencies that we  drive for example we're giving States  for the first time more control over  their own Medicaid money we'd really do  think it sounds like it's fairly  esoteric down in the weeds those types  of innovations allowing States to have  more control over their Medicaid money  drive tremendous long-term savings  director one of the things that the  Republican plan is does keep coverage  for pre-existing conditions let me ask  you about preventative care one of the  things that Obamacare did was expand  coverage and mandate coverage for  preventive care such as mammograms  contraceptive care also mental health  screenings will that continue under this  Republican plan yeah the way we get at  those things is a dramatic expansion of  health savings accounts so that you can  use your pre-tax dollars we double the  size of them are nearly double the size  of them and then dramatically increase  where you can use them so we do feel  that people will still have the ability  to get that type of care in a very  affordable fashion all right director  Mulvaney thank you for joining us thank  you

